<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tycho Brahe</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1546">1546</a>–<a href="/years/1601">1601</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tycho Brahe was a Danish nobleman and astronomer. His observation of a new star in 1572 challenged the belief that the heavens were unchangeable. From his observatory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uraniborg">Uraniborg</a> on Ven, granted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Frederick II</a>, he made the most precise naked-eye measurements of his time. In 1599 he became imperial mathematician to Rudolf II in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>, where his data passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-kepler">Johannes Kepler</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tycho-brahe.jpg" alt="Tycho Brahe" width="320" height="395" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portr%C3%A4tt_av_Tycho_Brahe_-_Skoklosters_slott_-_90153.tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ludvig Holberg, born in Bergen, wrote comedies and histories that founded modern Danish-Norwegian literature, and the astronomer <b>Tycho Brahe</b>, supported by Frederick II at his observatory of Uraniborg on Hven, made the most precise naked-eye observations of his age; Ole Rømer first measured the speed of light in…</blockquote><a href="/empire/denmark-norway">Denmark-Norway · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Rudolf II, Prague drew artists, alchemists and the astronomers <b>Tycho Brahe</b> and Johannes Kepler, and the Defenestration of Prague of 1618 set off the Thirty Years War, which reshaped Europe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Kingdom of Bohemia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Tycho Brahe</b>’s observatory of Uraniborg on the island of Hven, granted to him by Frederick II in 1576, produced the observations from which Johannes Kepler derived his laws of planetary motion.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-denmark">Kingdom of Denmark · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1572 <b>Tycho Brahe</b> observed a brilliant new star and showed that it lay beyond the Moon, and in 1577 that a comet did too, so that the heavens were neither unchanging nor made of solid spheres.</blockquote><a href="/movement/scientific-revolution">Scientific Revolution · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>Work began on <b>Tycho Brahe</b>'s observatory on the island of Hven, granted to him by Frederick II of Denmark.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-8">8 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
